@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Output Parameters - SQL Server";
}

<div class="container-fluid py-4">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold text-primary">
            Output Parameters
        </h1>

        <p class="lead">
            Output parameters allow a Stored Procedure to return a value
            back to the user or application after execution.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What are Output Parameters?
            </h2>

            <p>
                Output parameters are parameters that allow a Stored Procedure
                to return a value to the caller.
            </p>

            <p>
                Unlike input parameters, which receive values from the caller,
                output parameters send values from the Stored Procedure back
                to the caller.
            </p>

            <p>
                Output parameters are useful when a Stored Procedure needs to
                calculate or retrieve a value and return that value after
                execution.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Basic Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE ProcedureName
    &#64;ParameterName DataType OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN

    SET &#64;ParameterName = Value;

END;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The <strong>OUTPUT</strong> keyword indicates that the parameter
                can return a value from the Stored Procedure.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SIMPLE EXAMPLE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example: Return Student Name
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we want to create a Stored Procedure that accepts a
                StudentId and returns the student's name through an output
                parameter.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentName
    &#64;StudentId INT,
    &#64;StudentName VARCHAR(100) OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT &#64;StudentName = Name
    FROM Students
    WHERE StudentId = &#64;StudentId;

END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTE OUTPUT PROCEDURE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Executing a Procedure with Output Parameter
            </h2>

            <p>
                To receive the value returned by an output parameter, we first
                declare a variable and then pass that variable to the Stored
                Procedure using the OUTPUT keyword.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DECLARE &#64;Name VARCHAR(100);

EXEC GetStudentName
    &#64;StudentId = 101,
    &#64;StudentName = &#64;Name OUTPUT;

SELECT &#64;Name AS StudentName;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-info mt-3">

                <strong>Explanation:</strong>

                <br />

                The variable <strong>&#64;Name</strong> receives the value returned
                by the <strong>&#64;StudentName</strong> output parameter.

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         OUTPUT PARAMETER WITH INT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Output Parameter with INT
            </h2>

            <p>
                Output parameters can also return numeric values such as
                INT.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentAge
    &#64;StudentId INT,
    &#64;Age INT OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT &#64;Age = Age
    FROM Students
    WHERE StudentId = &#64;StudentId;

END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTE INT OUTPUT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Executing INT Output Parameter
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DECLARE &#64;StudentAge INT;

EXEC GetStudentAge
    &#64;StudentId = 101,
    &#64;Age = &#64;StudentAge OUTPUT;

SELECT &#64;StudentAge AS Age;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The returned age is stored in the
                <strong>&#64;StudentAge</strong> variable.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         MULTIPLE OUTPUT PARAMETERS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Multiple Output Parameters
            </h2>

            <p>
                A Stored Procedure can have more than one output parameter.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentDetails
    &#64;StudentId INT,
    &#64;StudentName VARCHAR(100) OUTPUT,
    &#64;City VARCHAR(50) OUTPUT,
    &#64;Age INT OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT
        &#64;StudentName = Name,
        &#64;City = City,
        &#64;Age = Age
    FROM Students
    WHERE StudentId = &#64;StudentId;

END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTE MULTIPLE OUTPUT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Executing Multiple Output Parameters
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DECLARE &#64;Name VARCHAR(100);
DECLARE &#64;City VARCHAR(50);
DECLARE &#64;Age INT;

EXEC GetStudentDetails
    &#64;StudentId = 101,
    &#64;StudentName = &#64;Name OUTPUT,
    &#64;City = &#64;City OUTPUT,
    &#64;Age = &#64;Age OUTPUT;

SELECT
    &#64;Name AS StudentName,
    &#64;City AS City,
    &#64;Age AS Age;</code></pre>

            <div class="alert alert-success mt-3">

                The Stored Procedure returns the student's
                <strong>Name</strong>, <strong>City</strong> and
                <strong>Age</strong> through output parameters.

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         OUTPUT PARAMETER WITH COUNT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Output Parameter with COUNT()
            </h2>

            <p>
                Output parameters can also be used to return calculated
                values such as the number of students.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentCount
    &#64;StudentCount INT OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT &#64;StudentCount = COUNT(*)
    FROM Students;

END;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         EXECUTE COUNT OUTPUT
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Executing COUNT Output Parameter
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DECLARE &#64;TotalStudents INT;

EXEC GetStudentCount
    &#64;StudentCount = &#64;TotalStudents OUTPUT;

SELECT &#64;TotalStudents AS TotalStudents;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The total number of students is returned through the
                <strong>&#64;StudentCount</strong> output parameter.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INPUT + OUTPUT PARAMETER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Input and Output Parameters Together
            </h2>

            <p>
                A Stored Procedure can use input and output parameters
                together.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE PROCEDURE GetStudentCity
    &#64;StudentId INT,
    &#64;City VARCHAR(50) OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN

    SELECT &#64;City = City
    FROM Students
    WHERE StudentId = &#64;StudentId;

END;</code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                Execute:
            </h5>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>DECLARE &#64;StudentCity VARCHAR(50);

EXEC GetStudentCity
    &#64;StudentId = 101,
    &#64;City = &#64;StudentCity OUTPUT;

SELECT &#64;StudentCity AS City;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning mb-4">

        <h4 class="fw-bold">
            Important Points
        </h4>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li>
                Output parameters return values from a Stored Procedure.
            </li>

            <li>
                The <strong>OUTPUT</strong> keyword is required when defining
                an output parameter.
            </li>

            <li>
                The <strong>OUTPUT</strong> keyword is also required when
                passing the parameter during execution.
            </li>

            <li>
                Output parameters can use different data types such as
                INT, VARCHAR, DATE and DECIMAL.
            </li>

            <li>
                A Stored Procedure can have multiple output parameters.
            </li>

            <li>
                Input and output parameters can be used together.
            </li>

            <li>
                Output parameters are useful for returning calculated or
                retrieved values to the caller.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary p-4 mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            Output Parameters allow Stored Procedures to return values
            back to the caller. They are declared using the OUTPUT keyword
            and can return values such as student details, calculated
            values and counts. A Stored Procedure can have one or multiple
            output parameters and can also use input and output parameters
            together.
        </p>

    </div>


